[{:seon.task/name "a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "DECISIONS 2026-11-15 (owner via root; reviewed by flow and deps; notes runtime-core-index-plan-2026-11-15.md and runtime-reactive-plan-2026-11-15.md). Owner: index and proofs first, fully proven, the least code for 90% of the value; the core lands WITH its first real callers, proved on them: the page walk's :readers (render/queue-what-moved!) moves onto the index in the same landing, measured on the standing root after web's stall fix (the walk's readers count and per-commit cost before and after, against the ~163M post-fix cold walk, never the 6.8B debug-tab draw); health's delta fold follows as the next landing on the same core. Two reader kinds: a delta fold (state + a commit's datoms -> state') for aggregates (health, laws, the reconciler index), and a reaction for point reads (a page's component). Feed: d/changes per owner (deps proved it complete: 0 of 40 differences were net changes [deps M result/d1d98078]; 5.8M against 26.2M ticks over 406 commits [result/aa42fbe8]); the writer's notice stays {:t :commit :program-t}, each owner reads its own change in its own step, :main is a sliding buffer (flow). MEASURED today's commit! [M root result/bcecbc16]: 53.6k / 538k / 4.41M ticks at 10 / 100 / 1,000 readers for a d=10 commit none read (~5.4k a reader); a hit reruns exactly its reader. Design (~25 lines in seon.ratom, nothing new in seon.db): the Store keeps {key #{reaction}} (seon.db/reads-add, reads-drop, readers) beside its watches and holds its owner; a reaction's run updates its keys in each Store it watches, dropping them on unwatch or dispose; a run whose *owner* is not the Store's sends {:seon.ratom/keys r ks} to the Store owner's port, never a direct set! (flow's hole: the same race is latent today in watches); commit! iterates the index value captured before its loop and wakes only (readers idx datoms); a watch that is not a reaction is still called; deleted: the per-reaction reads-touched? scan on a store change. Proofs, each red first: (a) a d=10 commit at 10, 100 and 1,000 readers within 2x (82x today); (b) a 10x larger store within 2x; (c) a hot [:a a] attribute read whole by 100 readers reruns exactly 100, the commit's own cost within its budget; (d) the rerun set equals today's reads-touched? scan, generatively, seeded with deps' cases (byte-array values, [:av] through a lookup ref or ident vs the eid, cardinality-one upserts and re-assertions, a schema datom (:all) and an attribute rename, fixnum key collisions), plus the stress run at root's REPL: main's last 100 real commits replayed into 1,000 real readers, one process, no load; (e) a cross-owner key update arrives as a message. An agent, not runtime, merges and tests it. The design below stays as the height-queue and owner-step background. THE PLAN item 11 (owner-approved order, 2026-11-15): the reactive core goes live, the render proc first. The index is seon.db/reads-add, reads-drop and readers (db.clj:1236-1260, written and tested, no src caller): each reaction's key set is added under it at its run's end and dropped at its next, replacing each reaction's own watch scan, so commit! is O(d): readers over the commit's datoms. The datoms come from the writer's commit notice (:ops), never a diff (agreed with deps). Render proc first: each view becomes a reaction over its store, fed by the render proc's :main notices. Deletes in that landing: the render proc's :read-index, reindex and touched (render/proc.clj:895-914), the walk's :readers and :db-keys (render.clj:351, 1258-1261), health's second diff (proc.clj:800). Proof kept: seon.render-test/a-move-of-main-is-diffed-once-for-every-walk. Estimate: per commit, d datoms x 5 keys x one map lookup (about 1k ticks at d = 12 [M, trial A1's prototype]) plus the readers' reruns; per reaction run, its keys' add and drop, O(keys). Health, the reconciler and test verdicts follow, each its own landing that deletes what it replaces (audit target/root/audits-2026-11-15/derived-values.md section 4). The plan below stays as the height-queue and owner-step design. Owner: runtime (owner 2026-11-14 21:20: runtime owns the reactive core). Analysis approved by root and flow (notes runtime-core-map/plan-advance-heights-owner-step.md). What exists [M]: seon.ratom/commit! notifies EVERY watch of the store and each reaction tests reads-touched? over the datoms: O(readers x d), 1.25M ticks for a commit no reader read at 1,489 readers (trial A1); seon.ratom/flush! runs its queue in arrival order and an auto-run reaction runs at once inside -handle-change, so a diamond's top runs once per path (3x, ctx-r1, A1). Design: advance! (store, new-db) diffs the held head and the new head once (datahike.db/datoms-diff of plain heads, never d/history or a view), looks each moved datom's 5 keys up in {key #{reader}} kept by each reader's run (keys gained added, lost dropped, never rebuilt whole; a schema datom touches every reader) and answers {reader [datoms]}; the flush is buckets by height (1 + max height of its reactive sources, kept on the reaction), always the lowest dirty reaction next, auto-run held to the flush; a source gained mid-flush with height >= the run's raises it and its ancestors (Jane Street Incremental's adjust-heights: state.ml add_parent, adjust_heights_heap.ml), moving queued nodes, and re-queues the run unnotified when that source is still queued; effects only from a settled run; a cycle is that run's typed :seon.ratom/cycle and the flush completes. Estimate: diff O(d log32 N) ~9k ticks and ~26 KB a datom at small d; lookup O(5d) ~1k ticks, constant in readers and N; index upkeep ~160 ticks a key gained or lost; flush O(r + h + sum a) (r re-runs, h depth, a ancestors raised); d = 10: ~0.6 ms. Deletes: commit!'s match over every watch, flush!'s arrival order, auto-run's run inside -handle-change. Proofs, red first where today fails: seon.ratom-test/a-commit-costs-its-datoms-not-the-readers (T = 1, 100, 10,000; d = 1, 10; :constant in T, <= 2 x 10k ticks a datom; a forced hash collision reruns the other reader, never skips the right one); seon.ratom-test/a-commit-costs-its-datoms-not-the-store (N 10^3 vs 10^5 within 2x); seon.ratom-test/a-diamonds-top-runs-once-a-commit; seon.ratom-test/a-reaction-that-gains-a-deeper-source-runs-once-with-its-final-value; seon.ratom-test/a-deep-chain-raises-its-heights-in-its-length (h = 100, <= 60k ticks); seon.ratom-test/a-cycle-made-by-a-run-is-that-runs-typed-error. See docs/reactive.md. The diff's cost (deps, Datahike B2, 2026-11-15 06:45 [M, root's main in memory]): datahike.db/datoms-diff of plain heads costs 87, 9 and 19 KB a datom for 1, 10 and 100 scattered datoms, 1.1 KB a datom for 100 clustered, and ~60 KB a datom at a merge's d = 7,163 (the whole index decoded on both sides). The alternative, a Datahike read of the commits' datoms in (from, to], was closed with runtime-2 (06:50): advance! keeps the diff of plain heads and no range read is added to Datahike. DEPS REVIEW (Datahike side, 2026-11-15, owner's ask): APPROVED with one condition. (1) The delta feed is the commit's own changes: in-process the writer's notice :ops, across processes Datahike d/changes [M result/dbef94e1, main's last 20 commits: 185k ticks, 0.9 MB, 174 datoms, against datoms-diff of the same values at 1.03M ticks, 3.8 MB]; a held head older than the log answers :datahike/changes-unrecorded and takes one diff, then follows. (2) No branch in a key: entity ids are store-wide, so one reads index per branch store. (3) as-of and since values are a cache by db reference, never a reaction; d/history is refused for reactions. CONDITION before O(d) is claimed: the index counted by key kind on real views, since readers under [:a a] (an attribute scan records it) or :any rerun on every write to a or every commit; if they dominate, record finer keys in datahike.reads (deps' fork) rather than accept the reruns."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ratom-test/a-commit-costs-its-datoms-not-the-readers seon.ratom-test/a-commit-costs-its-datoms-not-the-store seon.ratom-test/a-diamonds-top-runs-once-a-commit seon.ratom-test/a-reaction-that-gains-a-deeper-source-runs-once-with-its-final-value seon.ratom-test/a-deep-chain-raises-its-heights-in-its-length seon.ratom-test/a-cycle-made-by-a-run-is-that-runs-typed-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-owner-step-advances-its-store-and-flushes"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime; flow reviews (its bars: height order once, atomic effects with a between-reactions budget, cross-owner as port messages). What exists [M]: no production code feeds a store from real commits (the core map, notes runtime-core-map/0-who-benefits-from-the-reactive-core.md); the render proc, the reconciler, the fork server and each agent each diff main and filter by hand lists. Design: one step helper an owner proc calls: binds seon.ratom/*owner* and *tracks* once from its state (E2); takes its port's messages (a dirty from another owner, a resume); on the writer's commit notice advance!s its store (E6; an agent also from its own transact!); flushes by height with auto-run held; a reaction that throws keeps the throw as its value and the flush continues; a work budget, :seon.config.ratom/flush-ticks by owner kind (render, reconciler, agent, fork-server), worked from each owner's lower bound (one commit's typical re-runs x measured cost, x10), checked only between reactions (each effect atomic); spent, the rest stay queued in height order and the owner puts one idempotent {:seon.ratom/resume} on its own port channel (no flag: a throw cannot revert one; a resume on an empty queue is a no-op). Effectful reactions (loading code, the schema registry, config, contract re-arming) run in that step in root, the fork server and each agent. Estimate: overhead O(m + r), ~1k ticks plus ~300 a reaction run. Deletes (as each owner moves, per the map): writer main-commit's hand calls (load-changes!, hold-registry!, cfg/moved), the attr lists moves-program?, schema-moved, config-moved!. Proofs, red first: seon.ratom-test/a-flush-past-its-budget-resumes-on-its-next-step (with a step that throws after its resume is put); seon.ratom-test/a-reaction-runs-only-in-its-owners-step (flow's, kept green). See docs/reactive.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ratom-test/a-flush-past-its-budget-resumes-on-its-next-step seon.ratom-test/a-reaction-runs-only-in-its-owners-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-read-key-is-one-fixnum"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (root 21:58, 22:02). What exists [M]: a read key is a boxed Clojure vector [:ea e a] in a hash set, ~135 B a key (trial T9); a health class holds 1,522 (web T2). Measured on main [M]: 1,006 schema keys, the largest eid 1,152,920,505,196,681,680 (60 bits: Datahike's partitions use the high bits), so no split of kind + attribute + e fits a 61-bit fixnum. Rule: a key is one fixnum, a 60-bit hash of [kind a e-or-v], always, no partial packing, and STABLE across processes, versions and Jolt pins, since stored key sets are reused across them (root 22:06): e as a long through splitmix64 (pure arithmetic); a and v by our own FNV-1a-64 over canonical bytes, never Clojure's hash (not promised stable): a keyword's namespace and name as UTF-8, a v through the one codec (seon.edn/encode) to its UTF-8 bytes; a stored key set carries its format, :seon.reads/key-format 1, and a set of another format is read as no reuse; a collision only over-invalidates (the index finds the moved key's readers plus the colliding key's; it never misses); P(any collision) at 10^6 live keys ~ n^2/2^61 ~ 4e-7. A reader's key set is a sorted fxvector (8 B a key; gained/lost by a linear merge); the {key #{reader}} index is keyed by fixnum. Packed at capture and in advance! (deps may emit them from datahike.reads; else ~50 ticks a key in the core [H]). Estimate: 202 + 8k B a reader own, <= 100 B a distinct index key; 10k tracks at k = 10: ~3 MB own + ~8 MB index (boxed ~22 MB). Proofs: seon.ratom-test/a-read-key-is-equal-in-two-processes (GOLDEN values: literal fixnums in the test for a key of each kind, with a keyword, a string, a uuid and a map v, computed once when written; the core must compute exactly those, which holds across processes, restarts and Jolt pins; plus one fresh bin/jolt -e process over the same keys; a fork is no check, since it shares the hash seed (root); red under Clojure's hash); seon.ratom-test/a-track-costs-its-key-set-in-memory (k = 1, 10, 100: <= 2 x (202 + 8k) B own, <= 100 B a distinct index key, live heap after seon.lib/non-static-live!)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ratom-test/a-read-key-is-equal-in-two-processes seon.ratom-test/a-track-costs-its-key-set-in-memory}}
 {:seon.task/name "context-reads-on-the-core"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context; runtime and flow review core changes this needs (edited by context alone; root, 2026-11-15). The owner's call (2026-11-15): bring each area's hard case and today's number to the one reactive core, fix the core where it falls short, then move the reader and delete the area's own engine in the same land. context's readers are an agent's record (seon.ctx: build, patch) and its request (seon.ctx.wire over seon.render/walk's model output). TODAY [M root]: the record is already O(d): root's record builds cold in 1.3M ticks / 4.7 MB and a real 10-datom commit patches it in 3.9k ticks / 12 KB (result/b576d928); the request is not: root's session model output 6.8B ticks / 35.8 GB cold over 506k datoms [web-2 result/b9a43662], a 30-turn agent's cold request 11B / 37.6 GB [result/a444fa99]; the move must beat these. HARD CASES for the core: (1) ORDER and an append-only prefix: the request is an ordered conversation (turns by number, a message by its link to a turn, seon.ctx/placed-messages), and a provider caches a byte-identical prefix, so a reaction's output must keep its earlier members byte-identical and add at the end; a reader that rebuilds in hash order breaks the cache. (2) PAST POINTS: a turn's page message was drawn at two past points; landing 5 (jolt/context) stores what each turn sent, so only the turn being made draws, at its head: the core needs no as-of reads from context. (3) BIG FAN-OUT: root's pull reaches 506k datoms through history joins (every eval, every turn); an [:a a] key on :seon.eval/agent or :seon.model-call/agent would wake every reader on every turn; the record's windows (seon.ctx window-moves, :seon.config.ctx/window) are how it stays bounded today. (4) PATTERN EDITS: an agent changes its own pull (swap! of :seon.ctx/pattern) and the record is rebuilt sharing every unchanged subtree (seon.ctx/share); a refine compacts root's record by unlinking members (task seon.ctx/roots-context-is-compacted-by-a-refine-that-edits-its-record): the core must take a reader whose key set shrinks. (5) UNTRACKED reads: turn-entities reads written-once facts untracked (seon.ratom/untracked) so they never become keys. (6) COLD START: an agent's first request is a cold walk; today's 6.8B is the walk's own uncontracted code, not the recording (web-2's profile); measure ticks per recorded key on it before item 3. MOVE: the record stays a fold (seon.ctx/patch is one); the request becomes a reaction over the record's members, each member's messages a cached component; reader API and order from runtime's switch-over list. RUNTIME'S ANSWER (2026-11-15): the record stays a fold (seon.ctx/patch, O(d) at 3.9k ticks); the request is reactions over the record's members, each member's messages a kept component; the ordered append-only output is a delta fold over those components in member order, a new member appending and a changed one invalidating the suffix from it; shrinking key sets are the core's store-keys! (a reaction's next run sets its new keys, drops the old); untracked written-once reads are a gap until read through the recorder. NEXT (the design gate): prototype the request reader at the REPL on root's real session against today's 6.8B before any land; context's reader is item 5 of root's switch-over list."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-request-costs-what-its-commit-moved}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{request {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 10000000, :bytes 50000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}}
 {:seon.task/name "tests-reads-on-the-core"
  :seon.task/plan "TESTS' ENGINE AND HARD CASE (tests-4, 2026-11-15): test selection is a delta fold over what a commit moved: seon.program/reach-indexed (re-answers the tests whose :reads or :takes hold a moved input key, program.clj:2364) and seon.agent/test-plan (one derivation a plan), then the results branch's verdicts at each candidate's digest (seon.proofs/verdicts-at). A test is a reader whose read keys are datom keys since landing 12 ([:e form], [:av :schema/key k], [:av :config/key k], [:av :file/path p]: seon.proofs/read-keys), so 'which tests does a commit reach' is the core's (readers idx datoms) with tests as readers. Hard cases: (1) FAN-OUT: one schema entry moves 1,570 of 2,167 tests through seon.db/entity-index's whole-class read, one config key 1,670 [M root result/d9d2d387]; the index must answer a key read by ~1,500 readers without walking each (task seon.proofs/a-new-config-key-reruns-only-the-tests-that-read-it); (2) SIZE: 259k (test, key) pairs, 4,871 distinct keys, ~118 a test [deps M target/results]; (3) COLD START: the reach graph is built per process, 293-357M ticks and 1.4-1.8 GB [M result/b28d2a1d, result/a4c6e79a], so the index must be derived once and kept, not per plan; (4) HISTORY: a verdict is keyed at a past point (its basis), and reuse asks whether its reads still hold now (seon.proofs/reused-by-reads, reads-digest), a read of two points, not only of the newest commit. MEASURED today per change of main, at the pilot agent: test-plan 44.6M ticks cold, 31.4M warm, ~143 MB [result/a57de360] (221M / 848 MB before landing 12); its reach-indexed part 21.8M; the verdict seeks over 2,185 candidates 6.3M [result/dd4228f1]. The number the move has to beat: 31.4M ticks and ~143 MB a plan; the ideal is O(moved keys x their readers) with the read-key index held, a few M ticks for a 10-datom change. THE MOVE (root, 2026-11-15): the cleanup land of task seon.proofs/a-verdict-is-an-entity-that-names-its-environment is this reader's move, the owner's switch-over item 6: verdicts as proof facts carrying :seon.reads/key-set, deleting the verdict files, .recent, reads.edn and the reuse audit, in the same land. This entry is edited only by tests; the core's entry is runtime's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-plan-reaches-only-the-tests-that-read-what-moved}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.agent{test-plan {:n 2185, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 50000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}}]
